  "body": "## Bug\n\nAfter installing Qoder IDE (a VS Code fork), magpie shows **two** agent entries:\n1. \"VS Code\" — detected via the `code` command, which now launches Qoder\n2. \"Qoder\" — the Qoder CLI entry\n\nThe user only has Qoder IDE installed (VS Code was uninstalled). The \"VS Code\" row is a false positive — it's actually Qoder being misidentified.\n\n## Root cause\n\n`vscodeCodeBinary()` in `internal/agent/vscode.go` only excludes Cursor from the VSCode detection. Qoder IDE (a VS Code fork) passes the check and gets labeled as \"VS Code\".\n\nMeanwhile, magpie also has a separate Qoder agent entry (for the `qodercli` CLI), which reads its config from `~/.qoder/settings.json`.\n\n## To Reproduce\n\n1. Uninstall VS Code completely\n2. Install Qoder IDE (its installer registers the `code` command)\n3. Open magpie's agent list\n4. You see both \"VS Code\" and \"Qoder\" as detected agents\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nQoder IDE should be recognized as a single agent. It shouldn't show up twice — once mislabeled as \"VS Code\" and once as \"Qoder\".\n\nAt minimum, `vscodeCodeBinary()` should check `product.json` for Qoder's identity and exclude it from the VS Code row, so only the actual Qoder entry remains.\n\n## Additional context\n\nThe config directory for the VS Code row works fine — Qoder reads the same `Code/User` settings path. The issue is the duplicate entry and wrong label.\n",
